#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115337 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b898eab7-cbfc-5ce5-b03a-ed59d875416a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Function is Increasing"}] :question/id #uuid "514bbf6e-7eb5-5158-ac3f-5cb1f88d72d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1122" :question/problem "In which intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ (plotted below) strictly increasing? In which intervals is it increasing?\n\n![](images/cf/cfe907d96b0b9ee70fa48922b398baee66fe4bfded024134604bfb3a15c7c013.png)" :question/worked-solution "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that:\n\n- For $x\\in(-2,2)$ the graph rises\n- For $x\\in(2,5)$ the graph falls\n- For $x\\in(5,7)$ the graph is flat\n- For $x\\in(7,9)$ the graph rises\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n- The function is *strictly increasing* on the intervals where it always rises: $(-2,2)$ and $(7,9)$.\n- The function is *increasing* on the intervals where it never falls: $(-2,2)$ and $(5,9)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "badbc067-8e47-51ed-9a8c-40725fec2b49" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Function is Decreasing"}] :question/id #uuid "35bfc06f-0bc4-55b4-a037-49166721c3d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-1123" :question/problem "In which intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ (plotted below) strictly decreasing? On which intervals is it decreasing?\n\n![](images/d5/d5b32a598d6a0f4b8067ee98189d730e22042d87d38c96660191a7b7ecb9505c.png)" :question/worked-solution "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that:\n\n- For $x\\in (-4,-3)$ the graph rises\n- For $x\\in (-3,0)$ the graph falls\n- For $x\\in (0,2)$ the graph rises\n- For $x\\in (2,4)$ the graph is flat\n- For $x\\in (4,5)$ the graph falls\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that:\n\n- The graph is *strictly decreasing* on the intervals where it always falls: $(-3,0)$ and $(4,5)$.\n- The graph is *decreasing* on the intervals where it never rises: $(-3,0)$ and $(2,5)$."}]}
